.TH SYS_PLACE 2
.SH NAME
sys_place \- P-cores or E-cores for a thread (SYS_PLACE)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_place(int tid, int place);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
On a processor with P-cores (fast) and E-cores (efficient),
.B sys_place
says where a thread of the calling process runs:
.B PLACE_NORMAL
(P-cores first, for speed) or
.B PLACE_BACKGROUND
(E-cores first).
.I tid
0 is the caller, which moves at once if it is on the wrong kind; -1 is
the whole process, its later threads too; another thread of the process
moves from its next wake-up.
.PP
A thread is placed when it wakes: an idle core of its kind, else one of
the other kind; a normal thread may also ask a P-core running background
work to give way. Where all cores are alike, it has no effect. Processes
started with
.B SPAWN_BACKGROUND
.RB ( sys_spawn (2))
begin as background work.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0, or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I place
is neither of the two.
.TP
.B ESRCH
No thread
.I tid
in this process.
.SH BUGS
Tested in QEMU only, with the boot option
.BR pcores= ;
not yet on a real hybrid PC.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_info (2),
.BR sys_spawn (2),
.BR thread_start (2)
